WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.830
SPEAKER 1: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.787
hosted a plethora of talks
detailing the future of Cloud.

00:00:04.787 --> 00:00:07.120
If you didn't get a chance
to catch "Building Serverless

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.330
Applications with
Google Cloud Functions,"

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.280
stay tuned, because
here is the recap.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.149
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:18.440
There's a fancy new phrase
in the computing world known

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.410
as "serverless applications,"
which might be just

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.294
a little misleading.

00:00:22.294 --> 00:00:24.710
SPEAKER 2: See, serverless
doesn't mean not using servers.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.001
But instead it means that
you don't need to think about

00:00:27.001 --> 00:00:29.390
deployment, provisioning,
or managing of the servers;

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.150
that you're working in a
pay-for-what-you-use model,

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.190
where you deploy
functions, not apps;

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.530
and you're working in an
event-based or event-oriented

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.030
model.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.830
SPEAKER 1: To understand
why this is a helpful idea,

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.344
let's do a recap.

00:00:42.344 --> 00:00:44.510
SPEAKER 2: In a typical
cloud computing application,

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.110
you have to set up and provision
some VMs, which handle traffic.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.090
The number of VMs
you need is typically

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:51.620
a function of traffic volume.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.410
And in order to respond
to requests efficiently,

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.690
you typically have
to over-provision.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.210
This leads to wasted
money, since you're only

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.920
using those resources
in spiky scenarios.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.190
Now recently,
containers were added

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.140
to the concept of
cloud computing, which

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.200
allow you to deploy a
container on a VM, which

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.220
can handle traffic by itself.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.910
The difference here is
that as traffic rises,

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:17.750
you can allocate a new
container on the same VM rather

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.869
than a whole new VM itself.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:22.160
SPEAKER 1: However, you end
up back in the same problem

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.230
if you end up with
[? modulus ?] overhead

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:26.930
between the number of
containers and the number of VMs

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.260
you have allocated.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.069
Serverless is another way to
tackle this problem, where

00:01:31.069 --> 00:01:33.110
you break down your
application into the smallest

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:34.522
functions possible.

00:01:34.522 --> 00:01:36.230
You then deploy each
function and give it

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.120
the ability to handle capacity.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.710
Then let some
other system handle

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.840
provisioning and allocation
in order to reduce

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.670
over-provisioning costs.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.190
This is exactly the service
that Google Cloud Functions

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:46.910
provides.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.060
It's a serverless environment
to build and connect services

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.950
through code, which
means that you don't

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.210
need to think about servers.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.250
You only pay for what you use.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.530
And it's all event-oriented.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.120
SPEAKER 2: Here is
a small example.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.300
Internal and external services
can emit events, like changes

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.650
to a cloud storage bucket.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.180
And cloud functions
you've defined

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.680
will respond to
those events, which

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.320
can write back to the
cloud or call other APIs.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.780
Now, developing a cloud function
is really straightforward.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.654
Simply use your existing
knowledge of Node.js

00:02:13.654 --> 00:02:16.610
and Express frameworks,
deploy your HTTP or background

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.230
functions, and
you're ready to go.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.960
But the best part about all
this is that Cloud Functions

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.420
come built in with
Firebase and Stackdriver

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.190
support, which means
that you get access

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.070
to things like analytics,
database access, authentication

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.872
events, logging, monitoring,
and even an emulator,

00:02:30.872 --> 00:02:32.330
so you can develop
and test locally

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.674
rather than in production.

00:02:33.674 --> 00:02:35.090
SPEAKER 1: Check
out the recording

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.340
of Jason's full session
to learn more details

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.710
and see some great demos
for moving your existing

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.290
applications to
Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.080
And if you want more recaps
